# Security guardrails

Project hooks in `.cursor/hooks.json` enforce these rules. Do not weaken or remove them unless the user explicitly asks.

## Git

- Never run `git push --force` (including `-f`, `--force-with-lease`) to `main`, `master`, or any shared branch.
- Never run `git reset --hard`, `git clean -fdx`, or other history/worktree-destructive commands unless the user explicitly requested recovery from a named state.
- Never run `git config` (local or global).
- Never use `--no-verify` or `--no-gpg-sign` to skip hooks.
- Do not `git commit`, `git push`, `git tag`, or create PRs unless the user explicitly asked.
- Do not amend a commit that has been pushed.

## Shell

- Never run `rm -rf` on repo root, parent paths, or system paths.
- Never pipe remote scripts into a shell (`curl … | sh`, etc.).
- Prefer `spin` commands over raw `pytest`/`meson` (see AGENTS.md).

## Network

- Do not run direct network commands (`curl`, `wget`, browser/fetch MCP tools) unless the user explicitly asked.
- Indirect network via tools like `pip install` or `git pull` is out of scope for hooks; still avoid unless needed.

## Protected paths

Do not edit these unless the user explicitly asked:

- `.cursor/hooks.json`, `.cursor/hooks/*`, `.cursor/rules/security.mdc`
- `AGENTS.md`, `CODEOWNERS`, `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, `.gitignore`
- `.github/workflows/*`
- `pyproject.toml`, `requirements/*`

## Secrets

- Never commit tokens, keys, passwords, or `.env` files.
- Never paste secrets into chat, commits, or CI configs.

## CI / release

- Do not modify GitHub Actions workflows unless the user explicitly asked.
- Do not merge PRs or cut releases.
